<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Apple's privacy manifest for the app target.

  Derived from `docs/store/data-safety.md` §5, which cites the code behind every
  entry. Re-derive both together; a manifest that disagrees with the nutrition
  label is worse than neither, because the two are compared on upload.

  ## Two things about this file before anything else

  **It is not in the target yet.** A file on disk does nothing, Xcode has to add
  it to the app target's Copy Bundle Resources phase, and nothing outside Xcode
  can do that. Until an owner opens the project and adds it, this file ships
  nowhere. That is on `docs/store/release-checklist.md`.

  **The ad SDK landed on 2026-09-21 and this file has been updated for it.**
  GoogleMobileAds 13.9.0 is a package dependency, `GADApplicationIdentifier` and
  `NSUserTrackingUsageDescription` are in `Info.plist`, and `IOSAdNetwork` runs
  UMP then ATT then `MobileAds.start()`. So `NSPrivacyTracking` is now `true`,
  Device ID is tracking and third-party advertising, and Advertising Data is a
  new entry. That is `data-safety.md` §7.2 item 4, applied.

  ## Why `NSPrivacyTrackingDomains` is still empty

  Not an oversight, and not the same question as `NSPrivacyTracking`.

  Checked against the resolved SDK rather than assumed: GoogleMobileAds 13.9.0
  ships its own signed manifest at
  `GoogleMobileAds.xcframework/*/GoogleMobileAds.framework/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`,
  and that file declares **no** `NSPrivacyTrackingDomains` and no
  `NSPrivacyTracking` key at all. Apple aggregates the app manifest with every
  bundled SDK manifest, so domains Google connects to are Google's to declare.

  It also matters functionally. iOS **blocks** connections to domains listed
  here whenever ATT is not granted. Google's SDK expects to handle that case
  itself by withholding the IDFA and serving non-personalised ads, so listing
  their domains here would break ads for every user who declines rather than
  degrading them. Our own code connects to the config endpoint, Grafana and
  Sentry, none of which is a tracking domain under Apple's definition.

  ## The SDK half of the requirement is not ours

  Apple requires bundled third-party SDKs to ship their **own signed** manifests.
  This file cannot satisfy that on their behalf. Confirmed 2026-09-21 that
  `sentry-cocoa`, `GoogleMobileAds` and `UserMessagingPlatform` each ship one at
  the versions actually resolved. That closes §7.2 item 5.

  ## One thing this file does not settle

  The Google SDK's own manifest declares `NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeCoarseLocation`,
  because it derives approximate location from the IP address. It is declared
  there, so the manifest side is covered, but the **App Store nutrition label**
  is app-level and covers what bundled SDKs collect too. `data-safety.md` §2.9
  reasons only about *our* use of IP and concludes no location type applies,
  which under-reads the AdMob case. See OWNER-TODO.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<true/>

	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
		  The install id: a random UUID minted by CachedInstallIdProvider and
		  stored in AppData. Not the IDFA, not a hardware id. It rides on every
		  OTLP record and sits on the Sentry scope as a tag, so it is linked to
		  the device by construction, see data-safety.md §5, "the linked to the
		  user note", for why Linked is the conservative and correct answer.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<!--
			  Tracking since 2026-09-21. This row now covers two identifiers with
			  very different characters: our own install id, which is not tracking
			  and never leaves our processors, and the IDFA, which AdMob reads once
			  ATT is granted and which is. Apple has one Device ID row, so the
			  stronger answer wins.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeThirdPartyAdvertising</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!--
		  Which ads were requested, filled and seen. Entirely the Google Mobile
		  Ads SDK; no code of ours produces it. Tracking, because that is the
		  point of it.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeAdvertisingData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeThirdPartyAdvertising</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!-- Gameplay and funnel events. The registry is docs/practices/app-events.md. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!-- Sentry, via the sentry-cocoa Swift package. sendDefaultPii is false. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeCrashData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!-- Sentry performance traces and IosJankMonitor. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePerformanceData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!--
		  GrafanaLogTree forwards Warn-and-above KLog lines with the logger tag
		  and exception type/message. The body is whatever our own code logged.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherDiagnosticData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!--
		  Free-text feedback and bug reports, plus the session-log.txt attachment
		  that rides with every submission. The app asks for nothing identifying
		  and renders no email field, but the box is user-typed, and both stores
		  treat user-typed text as user content regardless of what it contains.

		  Purpose is AppFunctionality alone. Apple's purpose vocabulary is six
		  values: Third-Party Advertising, Developer's Advertising or Marketing,
		  Analytics, Product Personalization, App Functionality and Other
		  Purposes. There is no "customer support" or "developer communications"
		  among them, on either the manifest or the nutrition label. Play has
		  that purpose and Apple does not; do not carry Play's answer across.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!--
	  Required-reason APIs.

	  Two are declared and two known categories are deliberately absent:

	  - **UserDefaults** is real and ours. IosPreviousExitProvider persists the
	    MetricKit exit classification in NSUserDefaults.standardUserDefaults so
	    the next launch can report it exactly once. CA92.1 is the right reason:
	    access is limited to this app's own defaults.

	  - **File timestamp** is declared conservatively rather than because a
	    Kotlin call site was found. There is none: grepping iosMain for
	    attributesOfItemAtPath, NSFileModificationDate and friends returns
	    nothing. It is here because the Room/SQLite native layer and the
	    telemetry disk buffer both touch files the app itself created, and the
	    check Apple runs is on symbols in the linked binary rather than on our
	    source. C617.1 (files created by the app) is the accurate reason if it
	    fires at all. Over-declaring is not a violation; being surprised by an
	    ITMS-91053 email after a submission is a wasted day.

	  - **System boot time** is absent and that is a deliberate, documented
	    design choice, not an oversight. IosProcessStartTimeProvider is a no-op
	    precisely so the app never calls sysctl for a boot or process start
	    time; read its KDoc, which says so and says what the right replacement
	    is (MetricKit's MXAppLaunchMetric, which needs no declaration).

	  - **Disk space** is absent because nothing reads NSFileSystemFreeSize or
	    volumeAvailableCapacity anywhere in the tree.

	  If an upload ever warns about either absent category, add it rather than
	  arguing: the reason codes are C617.1 / CA92.1 style strings from Apple's
	  published table, and adding one costs nothing.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
